    projectBudget: 'Student time: ~12 hours. Compute cost: $0 (laptop). Documentation: ~3 hours.',
    projectTRL: 'TRL 3 -- Analytical demonstration of entanglement generation and verification with a two-qubit simulator.',
    questions: [
      { type: 'conceptual', question: 'What makes a quantum state "entangled" versus merely "correlated"?', answer: 'A state is entangled if it cannot be written as a tensor product of individual qubit states. Classical correlation means knowing one tells you about the other; entanglement means neither has a definite state individually, yet their joint state is perfectly defined.', explanation: 'Classical correlation: two balls in boxes, one red one blue -- opening one tells you the other. Entanglement: neither ball has a color until one is opened, but they always end up opposite colors.', commonMistakes: 'Confusing classical correlation (pre-determined) with quantum entanglement (jointly defined, individually undefined).', tip: 'The key test: can you describe each qubit independently? If not, it is entangled.' },
      { type: 'numerical', question: 'For the Bell state |\\\\Phi^+\\\\rangle, what is the von Neumann entropy of the reduced density matrix of one qubit?', answer: 'S(\\\\rho_A) = 1 bit (or 1 ebit). The reduced state is I/2, so S = -0.5 log_2(0.5) - 0.5 log_2(0.5) = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.', explanation: 'The von Neumann entropy S = -Tr(\\\\rho log \\\\rho) measures the mixedness of the reduced state. A value of 1 bit means maximum uncertainty about the individual qubit, which corresponds to maximum entanglement.', commonMistakes: 'Reporting entropy as 0 (the joint state is pure) instead of 1 (the reduced state is mixed).', tip: 'Joint entropy is 0 (pure state), but local entropy is 1 (maximally mixed). That contrast IS the entanglement.' },
      { type: 'application', question: 'Why can\\\'t entanglement be used to send information faster than light?', answer: 'Each individual measurement on one qubit of an entangled pair yields a random result (50/50). The correlation only appears when results from both sides are compared, which requires classical communication. The no-communication theorem proves this rigorously.', explanation: 'Alice measures her qubit and gets a random bit. Bob measures his and gets a random bit. Only when they compare notes (via phone, email, etc.) do they see the correlation. Neither can tell from their own results alone whether the other has measured.', commonMistakes: 'Thinking that Alice\\\'s measurement instantly "sets" Bob\\\'s qubit -- Bob\\\'s results still look random to him.', tip: 'Entanglement is correlation, not communication. The correlation is useless without a classical channel.' },
      { type: 'problemSolving', question: 'You have a source that produces pairs of qubits. How would you experimentally verify that the pairs are entangled?', answer: 'Perform a Bell inequality (CHSH) test: choose two measurement settings on each side, collect statistics, compute the S correlator. If |S| > 2, the pairs are entangled. Also compute the entanglement entropy from the reduced density matrix to quantify how much entanglement exists.', explanation: 'The CHSH test is the gold standard for proving entanglement. It rules out all local hidden variable theories. The entropy quantification tells you how useful the entanglement is for applications.', commonMistakes: 'Only measuring in one basis -- this shows correlation but not non-classical correlation.', tip: 'You need at least two different measurement settings on each side to witness entanglement.' }
    ],
